  "query_preview": "..Skies are clearing across the CWA with just some\nmid-level cloud cover east of Tucson. Aloft we are in a\ntransitional monsoon pattern with the ridge well to the east and\na closed low over the Pacific Northwest. There has been some \nreduction in moisture over the past day, but PWATs are still \naround normal. CAMs show a little more thunderstorm activity\ntoday compared to Tuesday with storm chances extending north\ninto Tucson and southern Pinal County. But greatest coverage \nand chances will still be south of Tucson. The 02/00Z HREF shows\na 20-50 percent chance for 1\"-1.5\" hourly rainfall rates from \nSells to Sierra Vista this afternoon and evening. This could \nlead to isolated areas of flash flooding with a secondary threat\nof strong gusty winds to 45 mph. Initial storms could form as \nearly as noon then continue an hour or two after sunset with \ngeneral motion to the north northeast.\n\nDown in the tropics, tropical storm Marie is centered about 450\nmiles south of the southern tip of Baja moving northwestward. It\nis forecasted to become a hurricane later this morning. As it\ncontinues its northwestward track tropical moisture will begin\nto be advected northward Thursday into the weekend. PWATs will\nlikely range from 1.4\" near the AZ/NM border to 1.8\" in western\nPima County. For Thursday this means expanded storm coverage \nnorthward with scattered convection over most of the CWA from \ncentral Pima County eastward. According to the HREF the heaviest\nrainfall threat will be from Tucson southward. The main threat \nagain will be heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding. \n\nThe tropical moisture sticks around through at least the\nfirst part of next week, which will lead to daily afternoon and\nevening thunderstorms capable of heavy rainfall. Expect area \nwashes and rivers to be running by Friday into the weekend, \nespecially south of Tucson where soils are already fairly \nsaturated. Aloft the closed low remains over the Pacific \nNorthwest through Monday, then begins to move inland thereafter.\nHowever ensembles bring another trough in right behind, so \nexpect Arizona to stay in a transitional monsoon pattern into \nnext week.\n\nOver the next week high temperatures will fluctuate between\nnormal and a few degrees below normal.",
